# One line. Run from the repo root by `orgasmic verify TASK-7QM8M`.
# `env -u ...` because a dispatch leaks its run id and home into subprocesses,
# and these tests build boards and drive the boot scan that must see neither.
#
# Three tests, one per claim the bounded read has to keep:
#
# 1. the bound itself — the boot pass must not read the middle of a session
#    file. Stated as something a whole-file read cannot pass rather than as a
#    timing: the live run's transcript carries a torn line deep in its middle
#    (a partial append, what `kill -9` mid-write leaves), so `read_session_file`
#    fails on it and drops the run, while the bounded scan never touches those
#    bytes. The byte assertion in the same test states the claim as a number;
#    the candidate assertion is what makes it a gate;
# 2. the truncation guard — a prefix window holding one run's acquire and a tail
#    window holding a LATER run's file end are two runs, and the unread middle
#    between them holds the first one's release. Reattaching it rehydrates a
#    dead run under a stale identity while the run that owns the end of the file
#    is not reattached at all;
# 3. the same guard's other half — `latest_run_segment` segments on `run_id`
#    alone, so two runtimes appended under one run id (the second-granularity
#    manager-file residue this function already guards against) are
#    indistinguishable once the gap hides the boundary.
env -u ORGASMIC_RUN_ID -u ORGASMIC_HOME cargo test -p orgasmic-daemon --lib -- the_boot_scan_never_reads_the_middle_of_a_session_file a_truncated_scan_never_reattaches_a_run_the_unread_middle_already_ended a_truncated_scan_never_combines_two_runtimes_under_one_run_id
